  "reasoning": "The patient presents with a high fever and persistent shivering/chills, indicating an acute systemic infection. Alongside these constitutional symptoms, there are distinct dermatological findings: rashes on the arms and red patches on the neck. Evaluating the differential diagnoses, malaria and dengue typically emphasize severe musculoskeletal pain, nausea, vomiting, or bleeding tendencies rather than prominent localized rashes. Impetigo primarily manifests as crusted, weeping sores concentrated around the nose and mouth. Allergic reactions usually involve respiratory distress, throat swelling, or generalized hives without high fever. Chicken pox, conversely, classically begins with fever and chills, followed by a characteristic vesicular or maculopapular rash that frequently emerges on the extremities (like the arms) and upper torso/neck. The specific combination of high fever, chills, and arm/neck rashes strongly matches the clinical profile of chicken pox.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]chicken pox[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}

